## 2. `README.md` (Project Documentation)

Save this file as `README.md` in your repository root.

```markdown
# DataVault (Java Edition)

DataVault is an ACID-compliant, zero-boilerplate embedded data storage system written in modern Java. It provides persistent key-value and semi-structured document storage backed by an embedded SQLite database engine.

---

## Features

- **Atomic & Crash-Resistant:** Utilizes SQLite via JDBC for ACID transaction compliance and write safety.
- **Schema-Agnostic:** Store plain strings, integers, flags, or stringified JSON payloads without manual schema migrations.
- **Built-in Auditing:** Automatically manages `created_at` and `updated_at` ISO-8601 timestamps per record.
- **Search & Categorization:** Full substring matching on keys, categories, and internal content with index acceleration.
- **Dual Interface:** Run directly as an interactive Terminal CLI or instantiate `DataVault` as an embedded class inside larger Java applications.

---

## Project Structure

```text
.
├── DataVault.java           # Complete Java core engine and CLI
├── index.html               # Responsive browser dashboard interface
├── sqlite-jdbc-<ver>.jar    # SQLite JDBC Driver
└── README.md                # Project documentation
```

---

## Prerequisites

- **Java Development Kit (JDK):** Version 11 or higher (`javac` and `java`).
- **SQLite JDBC Driver:** Download the JAR file from [Maven Central](https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/xerial/sqlite-jdbc/) (e.g., `sqlite-jdbc-3.45.1.0.jar`).

---

## How to Build and Run

### 1. Direct CLI Compilation

Place `sqlite-jdbc-<version>.jar` in the same directory as `DataVault.java`.

**On Linux / macOS:**
```bash
# Compile
javac -cp ".:sqlite-jdbc-3.45.1.0.jar" DataVault.java

# Run the CLI
java -cp ".:sqlite-jdbc-3.45.1.0.jar" DataVault
```

**On Windows:**
```cmd
:: Compile
javac -cp ".;sqlite-jdbc-3.45.1.0.jar" DataVault.java

:: Run the CLI
java -cp ".;sqlite-jdbc-3.45.1.0.jar" DataVault
```

### 2. Maven Integration (Optional)

If using a standard Maven project, add the dependency to your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.xerial</groupId>
        <artifactId>sqlite-jdbc</artifactId>
        <version>3.45.1.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

---

## Programmatic API Usage

You can embed `DataVault` directly into your business logic:

```java
import java.util.Optional;

public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Initialize vault (creates 'my_storage.db' automatically)
        DataVault vault = new DataVault("my_storage.db");

        // 2. Insert or update records
        vault.set("user.1001", "{\"name\": \"Jane Doe\", \"role\": \"Admin\"}", "users");
        vault.set("site.theme", "dark", "settings");

        // 3. Retrieve values
        Optional<String> theme = vault.get("site.theme");
        theme.ifPresent(System.out::println); // Output: dark

        // 4. Retrieve metadata
        vault.getMetadata("user.1001").ifPresent(item -> {
            System.out.println("Key: " + item.getKey());
            System.out.println("Category: " + item.getCategory());
            System.out.println("Last Modified: " + item.getUpdatedAt());
        });

        // 5. Query and delete
        vault.search("Jane").forEach(System.out::println);
        vault.delete("site.theme");
    }
}
```

---

## Web Interface (`index.html`)

Double-click `index.html` or open it with any modern browser to inspect, add, filter, and modify vault records locally via a responsive UI.
